course: language
lesson: 48
title: My dad could lift a car: playground boasting
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A boast is hyperbole about yourself or your people: «My dad could lift a CAR with one hand», «I'm the fastest kid in the whole WORLD», «I've been to Disneyland like fifty times».
explain: In the playground, boasts fly like footballs. Most are not lies meant to trick you: they show pride, love, or the wish to sound interesting to a new friend.
explain: So there are two traps: believing every boast, or calling the boaster a liar and starting a fight. The wise way is in the middle: enjoy the pride, and let the facts settle it — the chessboard, the score, a photo, a visit.
explain: And when the boaster corrects himself — «a MILLION Robux, well, 500» — the corrected number is the one that counts.
fact: A boast is a big claim about yourself, your family or your things, made to sound impressive; it is usually hyperbole, not a careful lie.
fact: «My dad could lift a car with one hand» means «my dad is strong» or «I love my dad and I want to win this argument»; «Your dad sounds strong!» ends it nicely.
fact: When someone corrects a boast, the corrected number counts: «a MILLION Robux, no cap, well, 500» means 500.
fact: «I'm the fastest kid in the whole world» after winning one race means «I'm fast and happy I won»; there is no world ranking for Year 5, and «Nice run!» is enough.
fact: «My house has like a hundred rooms» may mean a big house with perhaps five bedrooms; children boast to look interesting, and a visit will show you.
fact: «I've been to Disneyland like fifty times» might mean twice, and that he loved it; asking about his favourite ride is a friendly reply.
fact: Trash talk before a game, like «I could beat you with my eyes shut, easy peasy», is boasting for fun; the board decides, not the boast.
fact: In Liverpool, «our kid» means my brother or sister, «boss» means brilliant, «footy» is football and «defo» means definitely.
fact: «I swear down» is London and northern slang for «I promise», but swearing something does not make it true; a photo would settle «my nan's dog is bigger than a horse».
fact: «I've read literally every book in this library» means «I love reading»; a library has thousands of books, including cookery and car repair ones.
fact: In young people's slang «rizz» means charm, «aura» means cool points, «mid» means just average and «fr» means «for real».
fact: Facts can check a boast: a team that won 3–2 scored 3 goals, so no player in it scored 10.
exercise: «My dad could lift a CAR with one hand». What does the boy really mean? | His dad is strong, and he loves him | His dad lifts cars as his real job | He is lying on purpose to trick you | His dad is the strongest man alive
exercise: What is a nice way to end the «my dad is stronger» argument? | «Your dad sounds strong!» | «Your dad is a liar!» | «Prove it right now!» | «My dad's much stronger!»
exercise: Rosie says «I've got like a MILLION Robux, no cap, well, 500, but still». How many does she have? | 500 | 1,000,000 | 1,000,500 | 499,500
exercise: Malik wins one race in PE: «I'm the fastest kid in the whole WORLD». What is true? | He is fast in this class today | He is the fastest child on Earth | He has won a world championship | He is slower than everyone else
exercise: What is a good reply to Malik's boast? | «Nice run!» | «You're not the fastest in the world, actually.» | «I'm faster than you, I just didn't try.» | «Prove it in a race against everyone.»
exercise: Holly says «My house is massive, it's got like a hundred rooms». What is most likely? | Her house is quite big, maybe five bedrooms | Her house really has a hundred rooms | She lives in a palace with a king | She has no house and made it all up
exercise: Ethan says «I've been to Disneyland like fifty times». What does he mean? | He has been there, and he loved it | He goes there every single week | He works at Disneyland at weekends | He has never been there at all
exercise: Leah says «I could beat you with my eyes shut, easy peasy» before a chess game. What should Sam do? | Play the game; the board decides | Give up, because she will win | Get really angry and shout at her | Ask her to shut her eyes
exercise: A Liverpool boy says «Our kid's boss at footy». What does he mean? | My brother is brilliant at football | Our baby is the boss of the football team | The football boss is a young kid | Our goat is good at playing football
exercise: What does «defo» mean? | Definitely | Defender | Default | Different
exercise: Charlie says «My nan's dog is bigger than a horse, I swear down». What does «I swear down» mean? | I promise | I'm lying | Sit down | I'm angry
exercise: What would settle the argument about Charlie's nan's dog? | A photo of the dog | Charlie swearing louder | Asking the dog | Counting to a hundred
exercise: Leo says «I've read literally every book in this library». What does it show? | He loves reading | He has read every cookery book | He reads a book every minute | He is lying to get a prize
exercise: Jayden says «my rizz is unmatched fr, you lot are mid». What does «mid» mean? | Just average | Very cool | In the middle of the queue | Really funny
exercise: What does «rizz» mean? | Charm | Rice | Money | Speed
exercise: Ruby's team won 3–2. She says «I scored like ten goals today!». What is the most she can really have scored? | 3 | 10 | 5 | 2
test: «Ali's dad can lift a car with one hand. Should I tell him he's a liar?» What is the wise answer? | No, it's a boast that shows he loves his dad; just say his dad sounds strong | Yes, call him a liar loudly in front of the whole class so that he stops boasting | Yes, and ask his dad to come and lift a car to prove it | No, because it is a true fact that dads can lift cars
test: «Rosie's got a million Robux, she said so, I'm so jealous» What did you miss? | She corrected herself straight away: it's 500 | Nothing, a million Robux is what she has got | She said a million and then two million | She said 500 and then corrected it to a million
test: «Malik's the fastest kid in the world now lol, I'm so slow» What is a better way to see it? | He won one race today and is happy; it says nothing about the world, or about you | He really is the fastest child in the world and you are the slowest in your class | You should never race against Malik again because you will always lose | He is lying and the race does not count at all
test: «Holly's got a hundred rooms, can I live at hers??» What is the sensible reply? | Probably not a hundred; wait till you visit and see | Yes, she has ninety-nine spare rooms for all her friends | No, nobody can live in a house with rooms | Yes, but only in the room number one hundred
test: «Ethan's been to Disneyland fifty times and I've never been, it's not fair» What is likely true? | He has been a couple of times and it's his favourite thing | He goes every week and lives at the hotel | He has been fifty times exactly and has kept all the tickets | He has never been, he just saw it on telly
test: «Leah said she'd beat me with her eyes shut, so I'm not even gonna play» What is the best advice? | Play anyway; boasts don't move chess pieces | Don't play, she has already won the game | Make her shut her eyes before you start | Report her to the head teacher for cheating
test: «our kid's defo gonna play for Liverpool» A friend says «so he's signed for them?». What is right? | No, it's his brother's pride and a dream, not a fact | Yes, «defo» means the contract is signed and sorted | Yes, «our kid» is the name of a Liverpool player | No, because «our kid» means a baby
test: «Jayden called us mid and said his rizz is unmatched, I'm actually upset» What is the best way to take it? | It's a silly boast with new slang; laugh or say «sure, Jayden» | It's a real fact about everyone in the bus queue | It means Jayden thinks you are in the middle of a line | You should be upset, because «mid» is the worst insult there is
test: ★★ «I swear down», «no cap» and «literally» are added to boasts. What do they change? | Nothing: they make a boast sound surer but not truer | They turn a boast into a checked fact you can trust | They mean the speaker is telling a lie on purpose | They make the number in the boast ten times smaller
test: ★★★ Ruby's team won 3–2 and she says «I scored like ten». Her friend Zara says she scored two of the team's goals. If both girls are on the same team, what is the most Ruby could have scored? | 1 | 3 | 10 | 5
test: ★★★ Leo says he's read every one of the library's 4,000 books. If he read one book every day, about how many years would 4,000 books take? | About eleven years | About four years | About forty years | About one year
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion «My dad could lift a car with one hand», made during a dispute in a playground? | A boast expressing pride and affection rather than a measured fact | A deliberate falsehood designed to deceive the listener | A literal statement of the father's capabilities | A threat directed at the other party
exam: A pupil asserts that she possesses «a MILLION Robux, no cap, well, 500». Ascertain the quantity in her possession. | Five hundred | One million | One million five hundred | It cannot be ascertained
exam: Identify the meaning of the Liverpool expression «Our kid's boss at footy». | The speaker's brother excels at football | The speaker's child manages a football club | A young person employs the speaker | The speaker's goat is proficient at football
exam: It is given that a team won a match by three goals to two, whereas a member of that team asserts that she scored «like ten goals». Determine the greatest number of goals she can have scored. | Three | Ten | Five | Two
